WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:07.760
At exactly 6 AM on January 18th, 2014,&nbsp;
the sun began peeking above the horizon in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:13.680
Luanda, Angola, marking the beginning of what&nbsp;
seemed, at the time, like any ordinary day.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.800
Just seven nautical miles off&nbsp;
the coast sat anchored “Kerala”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:22.880
a Greek-owned, Liberian-flagged,&nbsp;
Angolan-chartered oil tanker.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.440
Kerala had just loaded&nbsp;
60,000 metric tons of diesel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.080
and was carrying a 27-person crew,&nbsp;
when something strange happened.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:39.200
The ship suddenly stopped broadcasting a signal.&nbsp;
Its owner tried but failed to make contact.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:46.400
As far as any map, satellite, or radar&nbsp;
was concerned, Kerala stopped existing.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:54.400
An entire day went by… nothing. Then&nbsp;
another day. And another. Then five more.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.240
Finally, on Sunday, January 26th,&nbsp;
eight days after its disappearance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.880
the tanker emerged 1,300 miles&nbsp;
north, off the coast of Nigeria.

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:11.440
It wasn’t hard to put the pieces together:&nbsp;
13,000 tonnes of oil, worth $8 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:17.200
was missing, one crew member injured, and the&nbsp;
ship’s identification markers painted over.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:22.160
In case there remained any doubt&nbsp;
that this was a clear case of piracy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.000
a suspicious tugboat had been sighted nearby.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Instead, the plot thickened…

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.760
Sponsored by Morning Brew — the free,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.600
daily newsletter that keeps you up to&nbsp;
date on the world of tech and business.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:42.800
When you hear the word “piracy”, you probably&nbsp;
think, consciously or otherwise, of Somalia.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.000
For a long time, this was the&nbsp;
capital of maritime hijackings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:55.360
At its peak, in 2011, 736 crew members&nbsp;
and 32 ships were held hostage.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:57.600
Why Somalia?

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.360
The answer seems obvious: The average person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:07.840
lives just 57 years, and its GDP per capita&nbsp;
is the second-lowest of any country on earth.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.880
It ranks at the bottom, in fact, of nearly&nbsp;
every metric where data is available.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.240
But while most of the population lives&nbsp;
on less than one US dollar a day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:23.760
just a few miles off the coast pass by ships&nbsp;
carrying many millions of dollars in cargo.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:31.280
To put things in perspective, Somali pirates&nbsp;
collected an estimated $70 million in 2009, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:36.480
would make it the country’s third most lucrative&nbsp;
industry, after Gold, and sheep and goats.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:43.520
The actual cost of piracy, on the other hand,&nbsp;
is much larger. It includes not only the ransoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:49.840
themselves, but also the second-order costs of&nbsp;
prevention, insurance, and lost efficiencies.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:55.440
When everything, from hired security to longer,&nbsp;
alternate routes are factored in, a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:01.840
hijacking, between Europe and Asia, increases the&nbsp;
overall maritime shipping costs that year by 1.2%.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.880
Now, around 2009, the world&nbsp;
collectively decided enough was enough.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:15.520
NATO’s Operation Ocean Shield was led&nbsp;
by the U.S., but everyone, from Norway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:18.560
to Pakistan, China, and even Russia pitched in.

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:23.840
It’s a strange set of circumstances&nbsp;
that gets India and Pakistan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:30.240
the U.S. and Russia, and China and Japan to&nbsp;
fight on the same side, but nobody likes pirates.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:34.880
And, to the surprise of no one,&nbsp;
guided-missile destroyers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:40.160
their assortment of Seahawk helicopters were&nbsp;
victorious over… a few dozen wooden skiffs.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:46.800
Since 2018, thanks to an aggressive&nbsp;
military response, both mercenary and naval,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.320
no attacks have occurred in the&nbsp;
international waters around Somalia.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:54.560
Piracy, it seemed, was&nbsp;
becoming a thing of the past.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.840
Then, like a game of whack-a-mole,&nbsp;
it sprung up somewhere else.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:04.320
This time on the other side of&nbsp;
Africa, around the Gulf of Guinea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:08.480
whose coastline stretches nearly&nbsp;
4,000 miles from Senegal to Angola.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:15.040
Today, a staggering 95% of all global&nbsp;
kidnappings occur in these waters.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.560
Eight of the nine ships fired upon in 2015&nbsp;
were traveling through the Gulf of Guinea.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.640
In 2017, over a third of all&nbsp;
ships passing through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:28.080
region carried kidnapping and ransom insurance.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.520
Although it hasn’t yet reached&nbsp;
the level of Somalia at its peak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.360
the Niger Delta is becoming&nbsp;
the new global piracy hotspot.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.880
Half of Nigeria lives in what the&nbsp;
UN calls “multidimensional poverty”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.440
which measures both income, and other&nbsp;
factors like healthcare and education.

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:51.600
Wherever there’s poverty plus&nbsp;
opportunity, it seems, piracy will follow.&nbsp;

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.720
Yesterday, Somalia. Today, Nigeria.&nbsp;
And tomorrow, somewhere else.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:04.800
Or, so it appears, on the surface. In reality,&nbsp;
what we’ve come to think of as “piracy”, is more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:10.400
a result of specific circumstances within Somalia&nbsp;
than the explanations Hollywood popularized.

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:14.640
Piracy in each region is&nbsp;
based on entirely different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.840
culturally and economically&nbsp;
distinct, business models.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:24.880
The central problem facing the country of&nbsp;
Somalia is that… it’s hardly a country, at all.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:31.360
Since the collapse of a centralized government in&nbsp;
1991, Somalia has been ruled by regional fiefdoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:36.480
with varying degrees of law and order.&nbsp;
Over the course of one 16 year period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.120
no fewer than 14 failed attempts were made&nbsp;
to establish a new national government.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:47.360
The result is that, while most of the world&nbsp;
recognizes only one sovereign “Somali state”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:52.400
there are, in effect, several self-proclaimed&nbsp;
independent governments and warlords.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:59.280
One of which, Somaliland, acts, in practice, like&nbsp;
any other nation — issuing its own passports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:02.480
holding its own elections,&nbsp;
and enforcing its own customs.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:08.240
This inability by the central government to&nbsp;
project power beyond the capital, combined with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:13.600
droughts, and aggravated by insurgents, means&nbsp;
that a general lawlessness pervades the region.

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:19.040
The resulting unemployment is one&nbsp;
of the primary drivers of piracy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:23.360
yet, Somalis have simultaneously&nbsp;
turned this into a strategic advantage.

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:31.120
Anyone, anywhere in the world armed with an&nbsp;
AK-47 and some bravery can attack a ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:34.960
most of which really only travel&nbsp;
at 20 or 25 miles an hour.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:42.720
The problem is what you do after that. The most&nbsp;
valuable cargo on a ship is its crew — from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:48.960
which you can potentially extract anywhere from&nbsp;
700,000 to nearly 10 million dollars in ransoms.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:55.280
But kidnappings are no walk in the&nbsp;
park. Fresh-faced pirates are promised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:59.680
action and adventure but soon discover&nbsp;
they’re more like small business owners.

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:06.080
Just as new franchisees realize all they’ve&nbsp;
done is paid for the right to flip burgers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:08.880
being a pirate isn’t all it’s cracked up to be.

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:14.080
Most of the job is spent transporting,&nbsp;
guarding, and feeding hostages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:17.840
doing bookkeeping, and,&nbsp;
above all… patiently waiting.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:22.880
For every one person holding a gun&nbsp;
announcing “I’m the captain now”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:26.480
there’s at least one more doing&nbsp;
the boring, grunt work on-shore.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:32.960
During the heyday, many coastal communities were&nbsp;
based entirely around supporting this “industry”.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:36.720
The average length of a&nbsp;
kidnap-for-ransom operation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.800
is 6 months, and some negotiations&nbsp;
drag on for over a year.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.000
That’s six whole months of just&nbsp;
waiting, trying not to get caught.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:52.640
But herein lies Somalia’s advantage.&nbsp;
Its complete and utter lack of formal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:57.200
institutions — a motivated police force&nbsp;
or functioning government — means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:02.720
its pirates can afford to hold hostages as long&nbsp;
as necessary without fear of being found out.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:05.840
Then there’s West Africa.

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:12.480
The region’s economic center is Nigeria, by&nbsp;
far the most populous country on the continent.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:20.720
Nigeria is an oil giant. Its over 600 fields&nbsp;
pump out 2 million barrels of crude oil a day.

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:24.720
The industry generates 95% of the country’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:28.160
foreign exchange and 80%&nbsp;
of its budgetary revenues.

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:31.840
Yet, in a classic case of the “Resource Curse”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:34.960
only about a quarter of its&nbsp;
oil is refined domestically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:40.960
The rest is shipped abroad, refined, and&nbsp;
returned in an expensive and inefficient cycle.

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:45.520
To avert protest against a system&nbsp;
that clearly benefits only faraway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:48.320
corporations and corrupt local politicians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:54.240
oil imports are heavily subsidized — making&nbsp;
its fuel among the cheapest in the world.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:58.400
Corruption, resource dependency,&nbsp;
and exploitation mean that, despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:04.480
producing more oil than anywhere else in Africa,&nbsp;
Nigerians lack reliable access to electricity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:08.560
instead spending $9 million a&nbsp;
year on fuel for generators.

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:14.800
On top of all this, oil importers regularly&nbsp;
decide they aren’t getting properly compensated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:18.000
cut off supply, and create&nbsp;
shortages across the country.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:23.360
The result is unmet demand for fuel&nbsp;
and a pervasive sense of injustice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:28.320
It’s hard to feel constrained by the law&nbsp;
when it’s abundantly clear that no one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:32.000
from local administrators&nbsp;
to top officials do either.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:37.040
One way this manifests is&nbsp;
illegal, makeshift oil refineries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:43.840
A group of… “entrepreneurs” will set-up camp&nbsp;
in the swamp, burn crude oil in open air pits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:47.440
and sell whatever comes out the other&nbsp;
end at gas stations across the country.

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:52.720
The other way is known as “petro-piracy”,&nbsp;
an extension of the widespread plunder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:56.240
of oil that occurs on rivers and&nbsp;
pipelines across the Niger Delta.

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:00.880
Unlike in Somalia, where most&nbsp;
pirate attacks are kidnappings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:04.800
a significant number here are&nbsp;
oil seizures targeting tankers.

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:12.000
In 2016, pirates stole 400,000 barrels of crude&nbsp;
oil every single day in the Gulf of Guinea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:16.400
for a total loss of $750 million in 2019 alone.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:21.520
Ironically, part of Nigeria’s&nbsp;
problem is the opposite of Somalia’s.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:25.920
Where Somali piracy flourished&nbsp;
in the absence of a state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:29.280
piracy in the Gulf of Guinea&nbsp;
is often enabled by it.

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:34.560
Put another way: its problem is that&nbsp;
it has too strong a formal economy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.400
and not enough lawlessness.

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:42.640
Recall the case of Kerala. Logistically,&nbsp;
it would’ve been difficult to hide a ship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:46.640
of that size for months at a time,&nbsp;
as was often done around Somalia.

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:51.280
On the other hand, some semblance&nbsp;
of formal institutions and markets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:57.200
are an asset for Nigerian pirates — there’s&nbsp;
always an unscrupulous buyer for stolen oil.

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:01.600
Petro-piracy is sophisticated. For starters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:07.440
it happens really fast — sometimes oil is&nbsp;
seized and re-sold in a matter of hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:11.520
quickly and untraceably moving between&nbsp;
the 20 countries in the Gulf of Guinea.

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:17.040
Attacks often require insider&nbsp;
information, connections, and planning.

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:21.280
Some studies suggest there’s&nbsp;
a kind of “sweet spot” between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:24.880
anarchy and order that maximizes&nbsp;
opportunities for piracy.

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:30.800
After a ransom was successfully paid for MV&nbsp;
Victoria, a freighter attacked around Somalia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:36.080
in 2009, for instance, pirates offered&nbsp;
to stay on board until it reached safety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:38.880
to ensure it wasn’t hijacked by a competing gang.

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:45.600
Similarly, when al Qaeda tried to set up shop in&nbsp;
Somalia during the 90s, it was frustrated by its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:52.480
lack of policing. Self-protection is costly and&nbsp;
time-consuming, even for pirates and terrorists.

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:58.160
When Kerala was eventually found, the&nbsp;
Angolan Navy conducted an investigation.

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:03.200
Its verdict? The whole episode&nbsp;
was faked. There was no hijacking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:06.160
and the suspicious tug was, instead, a “replica”.

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:11.120
What this means is either that someone on&nbsp;
the ship itself coordinated the attack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:15.600
or the Angolan government dismissed evidence&nbsp;
of what would be the most Southern attack in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:20.160
the Gulf of Guinea at the time, worrying that&nbsp;
it would deter shippers and harm the economy.

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:25.200
As you can see, the lines between&nbsp;
the formal and informal economies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:30.640
and the “good” and “bad” guys are blurred and&nbsp;
sometimes even change from one day to the next.

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:37.840
The lesson, if there is one, is that stamping&nbsp;
out piracy once and for all is nearly impossible.

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:42.400
Take the case of Somalia. While it’s&nbsp;
true that the robust, multilateral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:48.000
naval response was indeed effective, it&nbsp;
wasn’t quite as successful as it may seem.

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:54.320
Rather than disappearing overnight, pirates&nbsp;
and nearby ships, especially fishing vessels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.320 --> 00:13:00.160
reached a sort of mutual agreement: Pay us for&nbsp;
a “fishing license” and you won’t be disturbed.

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:05.280
Nigerians, likewise, conveniently,&nbsp;
though at times convincingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:10.160
argue that oil companies, who profit from&nbsp;
but give very little back to the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:14.880
are the real pirates — extracting value&nbsp;
from the land for the benefit of few.

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:18.960
As long as these fundamental inequalities remain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:24.560
piracy will only adapt in its outward appearance&nbsp;
— moving on land and changing in name.

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:30.240
…One thing that hasn’t changed much is&nbsp;
how we get our news. I can spend hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:35.280
scrolling through Reddit and Twitter and not&nbsp;
really remember anything. Today’s sponsor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

